export const DATA_62: ReadingTest[] = [
  { id: "rd-081", num: 81, title: "Đề 081", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "Vietnam: A Country of Culture and Change", paragraphs: ["Vietnam is a long, narrow country located on the eastern edge of mainland Southeast Asia, stretching from the mountains of the north to the fertile river deltas of the south. With a population of over ninety million people and a history spanning more than four thousand years, Vietnam is one of the most culturally rich and geographically diverse nations in the region. In recent decades, the country has undergone dramatic economic and social transformation, emerging from decades of conflict and isolation to become one of the fastest-growing economies in Asia. Yet despite these rapid changes, Vietnam has retained a deep attachment to its traditions, customs, and sense of national identity.", "The Vietnamese language is one of the country's most distinctive cultural features. It is a tonal language, meaning that the same syllable can carry up to six different meanings depending on the tone in which it is spoken. Although the modern writing system uses the Latin alphabet — a legacy of Portuguese and French missionaries who developed it in the seventeenth century — the language itself belongs to the Austroasiatic language family and has been spoken in the region for thousands of years. Vietnamese is the official language of the country and is spoken as a first language by the large majority of the population, though Vietnam is also home to more than fifty recognised ethnic minority groups, each with their own distinct languages and cultural traditions.", "Food is central to Vietnamese culture and daily life. Vietnamese cuisine is celebrated worldwide for its freshness, variety, and balance of flavours — combining herbs, vegetables, rice, and noodles with fish, meat, and a range of aromatic spices. Some of the country's most famous dishes, such as phở — a fragrant noodle soup — and bánh mì — a sandwich that reflects the country's French colonial heritage — have become popular in cities around the world. Meals in Vietnam are typically shared affairs, with multiple dishes placed at the centre of the table for everyone to enjoy together, reflecting the importance of family and community in Vietnamese social life. Street food culture is also deeply embedded in Vietnamese daily routines, with vendors selling freshly prepared meals from early morning to late at night in every city, town, and village.", "Vietnam's history has been shaped by a long series of foreign influences and struggles for independence. For over a thousand years, much of the country was under Chinese rule, a period that left lasting marks on Vietnamese art, philosophy, governance, and religion. The country later experienced French colonial rule from the mid-nineteenth century until 1954, followed by decades of conflict that culminated in the reunification of North and South Vietnam in 1975. Today, reminders of this complex history are visible in the country's architecture, museums, and festivals. Despite the hardships of the past, the Vietnamese people are widely regarded as resilient, forward-looking, and proud of their cultural heritage.", "Religion and spirituality play an important role in the lives of many Vietnamese people. Buddhism, which arrived from India and China over two thousand years ago, is the most widely practised religion and has profoundly shaped Vietnamese art, architecture, and ethical values. Confucianism, introduced from China, has historically influenced family structures, social hierarchies, and the importance placed on education and respect for elders. Many Vietnamese people also practise traditional ancestor worship — the belief that the spirits of deceased family members continue to watch over and protect the living — which is expressed through offerings, rituals, and the maintenance of family altars in the home.", "In terms of economic development, Vietnam has made remarkable progress since the introduction of the Đổi Mới economic reforms in 1986, which shifted the country from a centrally planned economy toward a market-oriented system. Since then, millions of Vietnamese people have been lifted out of poverty, literacy rates have risen sharply, and the country has attracted significant foreign investment in manufacturing, technology, and tourism. The cities of Hanoi in the north and Ho Chi Minh City in the south are now vibrant metropolitan centres, home to modern skyscrapers, international businesses, and a young, increasingly well-educated population. Yet rapid urbanisation has also brought new challenges, including traffic congestion, air pollution, and growing inequality between urban and rural communities.", "Looking ahead, Vietnam faces both exciting opportunities and significant challenges as it continues to develop. Maintaining its rich cultural identity while embracing globalisation, managing environmental pressures including rising sea levels that threaten the Mekong Delta, and ensuring that economic growth benefits all sections of society are among the most pressing priorities for the country in the coming decades. For the millions of visitors who travel to Vietnam each year, it remains one of the most captivating destinations in the world — a place where ancient temples stand alongside bustling modern cities, and where the warmth and hospitality of the Vietnamese people leave a lasting impression."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main topic of the passage?", options: ["A travel guide for tourists planning to visit Vietnam for the first time", "A broad overview of Vietnam's culture, history, religion, and economic development", "An argument that Vietnam should do more to preserve its cultural traditions in the face of globalisation", "A comparison of life in northern and southern Vietnam before and after reunification"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what makes Vietnamese a tonal language?", options: ["Each of Vietnam's ethnic minority groups speaks a different tonal dialect of the main language", "It belongs to the Austroasiatic language family, which uses tones to distinguish grammar rules", "It is written using a script that was developed by Portuguese and French missionaries", "The same syllable can have up to six different meanings depending on the tone used"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following best describes how meals are typically shared in Vietnam?", options: ["Each person orders and eats their own individual dish, reflecting a preference for personal choice", "Multiple dishes are placed at the centre of the table for everyone to enjoy together", "Formal meals are reserved for special occasions, while daily eating is done individually", "Meals are usually eaten quickly at street stalls rather than at home with family"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what happened in Vietnam in 1975?", options: ["North and South Vietnam were reunified into a single country after years of conflict", "Vietnam began a period of economic reform that opened the country to foreign investment", "Vietnam gained independence from France after decades of colonial rule", "Vietnam became a member of the Association of Southeast Asian Nations"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a religious or spiritual tradition practised in Vietnam?", options: ["Confucianism, which influenced family structures and respect for elders", "Christianity, introduced by European missionaries during the colonial period", "Ancestor worship, expressed through offerings and family altars in the home", "Buddhism, which shaped Vietnamese art, architecture, and ethical values"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what was one result of the Đổi Mới economic reforms introduced in 1986?", options: ["Vietnam shifted from a market economy back to a centrally planned system", "Millions of Vietnamese people were lifted out of poverty and literacy rates improved significantly", "The government restricted foreign investment to protect domestic industries from competition", "Rural communities grew faster than cities as farming became more productive and profitable"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"their\" in paragraph 2 refers to _______.", options: ["more than fifty recognised ethnic minority groups living in Vietnam", "Portuguese and French missionaries who developed the Latin writing system", "members of the Austroasiatic language family across Southeast Asia", "the large majority of the population who speak Vietnamese as a first language"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"it\" in paragraph 7 refers to _______.", options: ["the cultural identity that Vietnam has worked hard to preserve", "the Mekong Delta, which is threatened by rising sea levels", "Vietnam, described as one of the most captivating destinations in the world", "Ho Chi Minh City, which has grown into a major international tourist hub"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"worship\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["the study of religious texts and philosophical writings", "the practice of showing deep respect or devotion to a spiritual figure or being", "a legal agreement between family members about the management of property", "a formal ceremony held only during national public holidays"], answer: 1 }, { q: "What is the author's overall attitude toward Vietnam?", options: ["Negative — the author suggests that Vietnam's cultural traditions are being lost to modernisation", "Neutral — the author presents facts about Vietnam without expressing any personal opinion", "Admiring — the author presents Vietnam as a country with a rich heritage and impressive resilience", "Critical — the author focuses mainly on the social problems and inequalities created by rapid economic growth"], answer: 2 }] },
    { title: "Why Some Children Become Picky Eaters", paragraphs: ["Almost every parent has experienced the frustration of watching a child push a perfectly nutritious meal to the side of the plate, refuse a food that was happily eaten just days before, or insist on eating the same three or four familiar dishes day after day. Picky eating — the tendency to reject unfamiliar or disliked foods — is one of the most common concerns raised by parents with young children, and it can be a source of considerable stress at mealtimes. While most children pass through a phase of selective eating, for some the pattern persists well into adolescence and beyond. Understanding the factors that make some children more prone to picky eating than others requires examining a combination of biological, psychological, and environmental influences.", "One of the most important biological factors is a condition known as food neophobia — literally, the fear of new foods. Research in developmental psychology suggests that food neophobia typically peaks between the ages of two and six, a period when young children begin to have more independence but not yet the cognitive maturity to evaluate unfamiliar things with confidence. From an evolutionary perspective, this wariness toward new foods may have served a protective function: in environments where food could be poisonous or contaminated, caution about eating unfamiliar substances was a survival advantage. Studies of twins have consistently shown that food neophobia has a significant heritable component, meaning that a child's tendency to avoid new foods is partly determined by genetics rather than solely by upbringing or experience.", "Taste sensitivity plays an equally significant role. Some individuals — known as \"supertasters\" — have a higher density of taste receptors on their tongues than average, making them more intensely sensitive to bitter, sour, and spicy flavours. Children who are supertasters may find vegetables such as broccoli, spinach, or Brussels sprouts overwhelmingly bitter, while adults with average taste sensitivity barely notice the bitterness at all. This heightened sensitivity is not a matter of preference or behaviour; it is a physiological difference that makes certain foods genuinely more unpleasant for the children who experience them . Parents who are unaware of this biological basis may misinterpret their child's refusals as stubbornness or manipulation, leading to conflict that can make mealtimes more stressful and further reinforce the child's negative associations with food.", "Texture is another dimension that is frequently underestimated by adults when trying to understand picky eating. While most adults judge foods primarily by their taste and smell, many children are highly sensitive to the physical properties of what they eat — particularly the texture in the mouth, which researchers sometimes call \"mouthfeel.\" Foods with mixed textures, such as soups containing both liquid and solid elements, or casseroles in which the components have merged together, can be especially challenging for texture-sensitive children, who may experience the combination as deeply unpleasant. Occupational therapists who work with children with significant feeding difficulties note that texture sensitivity frequently co-occurs with heightened sensory sensitivity in other areas of life, and may in some cases be linked to conditions such as sensory processing disorder or autism spectrum disorder.", "Psychological and social factors shape picky eating just as powerfully as biology. Children are highly attuned to the emotional climate of mealtimes and can develop lasting negative associations with foods that were introduced during stressful or pressured eating experiences. Research has shown that parents who frequently pressure children to eat — using tactics such as rewards, threats, or the \"clean your plate\" rule — tend to produce children who are less willing to try new foods and less able to recognise and respond to their own internal hunger and fullness signals. By contrast, a feeding approach known as \"division of responsibility,\" developed by dietitian Ellyn Satter, proposes that parents decide what food is offered and when, while children are trusted to decide whether and how much to eat — an approach that research has linked to more relaxed mealtimes and greater dietary diversity over time.", "Repeated exposure is widely recognised as the most effective strategy for expanding a child's food acceptance. Studies suggest that a child may need to encounter a new food between ten and fifteen times before accepting it willingly — a far higher number than most parents expect or persist with. The exposure does not always need to involve tasting; simply being present when a food is being prepared, touching it, or seeing other family members eat it with enjoyment can gradually reduce the anxiety that unfamiliar foods trigger. Serving new foods alongside familiar favourites, offering very small portions without pressure, and maintaining a calm and positive atmosphere at mealtimes are all strategies that research supports as effective for gradually widening the range of foods children are willing to eat.", "Most children who are picky eaters during the early years do grow to accept a wider variety of foods as they mature, particularly when parents respond with patience and consistency rather than anxiety or conflict. However, for a smaller number of children whose food refusal is severe enough to cause nutritional deficiencies, significant weight loss, or extreme distress, professional support from a paediatrician, dietitian, or feeding therapist may be necessary. Recognising the difference between typical developmental picky eating and a more clinically significant feeding difficulty is an important step in ensuring that children receive the right level of support at the right time."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main purpose of the passage?", options: ["To examine the biological, psychological, and environmental reasons why some children become picky eaters", "To argue that picky eating is primarily caused by poor parenting choices during early childhood", "To advise parents on the best meal plans for children who refuse to eat vegetables", "To compare different medical treatments available for children with severe feeding difficulties"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"tendency\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["a temporary phase that all children go through and then grow out of", "a conscious decision made by a child to avoid certain types of food", "a strict rule that applies to all children in the same way", "a natural inclination or predisposition toward a particular behaviour"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, why do some children find vegetables like broccoli or Brussels sprouts unpleasant?", options: ["Supertaster children have more taste receptors and experience the bitterness of these vegetables far more intensely", "Children learn to dislike these vegetables by observing negative reactions from adults at mealtimes", "Young children's taste buds have not yet developed enough to appreciate the complex flavours of green vegetables", "These vegetables contain chemicals that cause allergic reactions in children with sensitive immune systems"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what type of food is described as particularly challenging for texture-sensitive children?", options: ["Foods with mixed textures, such as soups containing both liquid and solid elements", "Foods with strong smells, which texture-sensitive children tend to associate with unpleasant experiences", "Foods that are very dry and require a lot of chewing, such as crackers and raw carrots", "Foods that are very cold or very hot, which can trigger discomfort in sensitive taste receptors"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is the key principle behind Ellyn Satter's \"division of responsibility\" approach?", options: ["Children are required to eat a set portion of every food on their plate before being excused from the table", "Responsibility for cooking meals is gradually transferred to children as they grow older and become more independent", "Parents are responsible for deciding what food is offered and when, while children decide whether and how much to eat", "Parents and children take turns selecting which foods are served at each meal throughout the week"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, how many times may a child need to encounter a new food before accepting it willingly?", options: ["Between fifteen and twenty times", "Between five and ten times", "Between three and five times", "Between ten and fifteen times"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a way to help children become comfortable with new foods?", options: ["Offering very small portions of new foods without applying pressure", "Allowing children to be present when the food is being prepared", "Promising children a reward such as a dessert if they finish a new food completely", "Letting children see family members eat the new food with enjoyment"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"them\" in paragraph 3 refers to _______.", options: ["parents who misinterpret their child's food refusals as deliberate stubbornness", "taste receptors that are more densely packed on the tongues of supertasters", "children who have been identified as supertasters by researchers in developmental psychology", "certain foods that are made genuinely more unpleasant by a child's heightened taste sensitivity"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"climate\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["the variety of food options available to children during a typical family meal", "the temperature and physical conditions of the room where meals are eaten", "the overall emotional atmosphere or mood surrounding a particular situation", "the set of rules and expectations that parents establish around mealtimes"], answer: 2 }, { q: "What can be inferred from the final paragraph about the author's intended message for parents?", options: ["The author implies that most parents make their child's picky eating worse by being too relaxed about food refusals", "The author implies that patience, consistency, and knowing when to seek help are the most important tools available to parents", "The author concludes that picky eating is an inevitable problem that cannot be meaningfully improved through parental strategies", "The author suggests that professional intervention is almost always required for children who go through a picky eating phase"], answer: 1 }] },
    { title: "The Growing Problem of Plastic Waste", paragraphs: ["Plastic has become one of the defining materials of modern civilisation. Lightweight, durable, inexpensive, and endlessly versatile, it has revolutionised industries from food packaging and medicine to construction and electronics. Yet the very properties that make plastic so useful — its resistance to decay and its structural stability — also make it one of the most persistent environmental pollutants in history. Since large-scale plastic production began in the 1950s, an estimated 8.3 billion metric tonnes of plastic have been manufactured worldwide, and of that vast quantity, only about nine percent has ever been recycled. The remainder has been incinerated, buried in landfills, or released into the natural environment, where it can persist for hundreds of years without breaking down.", "The scale of plastic pollution in the world's oceans has attracted particular scientific and public attention. Researchers estimate that between eight and twelve million metric tonnes of plastic enter the ocean every year, carried there by rivers, storm drains, and coastal littering. Once in the ocean, plastic does not disappear; instead, it is broken down by sunlight and wave action into progressively smaller pieces called microplastics — fragments less than five millimetres in size. These tiny particles have now been detected in every ocean on Earth, from the surface waters of tropical seas to the sediments of the deep ocean floor, and even in the bodies of fish, seabirds, marine mammals, and zooplankton at the base of the food chain. The ecological consequences of this pervasive contamination are still being studied, but early findings raise serious concerns about the impact on reproductive health, immune function, and behaviour across a wide range of marine species.", "Land-based plastic pollution presents a different but equally serious set of problems. In many low- and middle-income countries, waste management infrastructure is insufficient to handle the volumes of plastic being generated, and significant quantities end up in open dumps or illegal disposal sites where they are exposed to weather, animals, and open burning. The toxic gases produced by burning plastic — including dioxins and furans — pose direct health risks to communities living nearby, particularly affecting children and pregnant women. Even in high-income countries with more developed waste management systems, the recycling of plastic remains technically challenging and economically constrained: many types of plastic are difficult or impossible to recycle profitably at scale, and contamination of recycling streams frequently renders entire batches of otherwise recyclable material unusable.", "The economic costs of plastic pollution are increasingly being quantified. A 2016 report by the Ellen MacArthur Foundation estimated that plastic pollution costs the global economy approximately eighty billion dollars annually through its impact on fisheries, tourism, and the cost of managing plastic waste in coastal areas. [1] Fisheries suffer when marine animals ingest or become entangled in plastic debris, reducing fish stocks and the livelihoods of fishing communities. [2] Tourism revenues are depressed in areas where beaches and coastal waters are visibly contaminated. [3] The costs of removing plastic from ocean gyres — the large rotating systems of ocean currents where plastic accumulates in enormous concentrations — are prohibitively high, leading most experts to conclude that prevention is far more cost-effective than cleanup. [4]", "Efforts to address plastic pollution have taken a variety of forms at both the national and international level. More than sixty countries have introduced some form of restriction on single-use plastics — the bags, straws, cutlery, and packaging items that are used briefly and then discarded. The European Union has implemented some of the world's most comprehensive single-use plastics legislation, which came into force in 2021 and bans a wide range of disposable plastic products while requiring that plastic bottles be made with a minimum proportion of recycled content. Extended producer responsibility schemes, which require manufacturers to bear the cost of collecting and recycling the packaging they place on the market, have also been introduced in a growing number of jurisdictions and represent a significant shift of financial responsibility from governments and consumers to producers.", "Innovation in materials science offers some promise. Biodegradable and compostable plastics — materials designed to break down more rapidly under specific conditions — have attracted considerable investment, though their environmental benefits in practice are more limited than their proponents sometimes suggest. Most biodegradable plastics require industrial composting facilities to break down effectively; if they end up in the ocean or a landfill, they behave much like conventional plastic. A more fundamental challenge is the question of whether it is possible to design a circular economy for plastic — a system in which plastic materials are continuously recovered and reused rather than discarded — given the enormous diversity of plastic types in use and the technical limitations of current recycling technology.", "Ultimately, reducing the environmental impact of plastic will require action across the entire lifecycle of the material — from the design of products and packaging, through production, use, and collection, to the development of genuinely effective end-of-life solutions. No single policy or technology will be sufficient. The evidence increasingly suggests that the most effective strategies combine strict regulation of the most problematic plastic products, investment in waste management infrastructure, extended producer responsibility, and sustained support for materials innovation — pursued simultaneously and at a scale commensurate with the size of the problem."], questions: [{ q: "What is the central focus of the passage?", options: ["A scientific explanation of how microplastics form and spread through ocean currents", "A comprehensive examination of the scale, consequences, and potential solutions to plastic pollution", "A comparison of plastic recycling systems in high-income and low-income countries", "An argument that plastic production should be banned entirely within the next decade"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what are microplastics?", options: ["Invisible chemical residues left behind when plastic packaging is heated or incinerated", "Plastic fragments smaller than five millimetres produced when larger pieces break down in the ocean", "A special type of plastic designed to dissolve harmlessly in seawater over time", "Plastic pellets used as raw material in the industrial manufacture of plastic products"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a problem caused by land-based plastic pollution?", options: ["Groundwater contamination caused by plastic chemicals leaching into soil beneath landfills", "Insufficient waste management infrastructure in many lower-income countries", "The difficulty and expense of recycling many types of plastic profitably", "Toxic gases released by the open burning of plastic waste"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, why do most experts consider prevention more practical than cleanup for ocean plastic?", options: ["Because cleanup technologies have not yet been invented and no research programmes currently exist", "Because the cost of removing plastic from ocean gyres where it collects is prohibitively high", "Because cleaning up ocean plastic would damage fragile marine ecosystems more than leaving it in place", "Because international law prohibits the use of ships and equipment in ocean gyres where plastic accumulates"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what does the European Union's single-use plastics legislation require of plastic bottle manufacturers?", options: ["That all plastic bottles be replaced with glass or metal containers within five years of the legislation taking effect", "That plastic bottles include a minimum proportion of recycled content in their construction", "That all plastic bottles carry a deposit scheme label to encourage consumers to return them for recycling", "That manufacturers fund public awareness campaigns about the importance of recycling plastic bottles"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, under what conditions do biodegradable plastics actually break down effectively?", options: ["In warm ocean water, where salt and sunlight accelerate the chemical breakdown process", "When buried in soil at a depth of at least one metre, away from exposure to weather and animals", "Automatically within six months of manufacture, regardless of the environment in which they are disposed of", "Only in industrial composting facilities, not in natural environments like oceans or landfills"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"These\" in paragraph 2 refers to _______.", options: ["the sunlight and wave action that break larger plastic items into smaller particles", "microplastic fragments smaller than five millimetres produced by the breakdown of larger plastic pieces", "the tropical seas and deep ocean sediments where plastic pollution has been found", "fish, seabirds, and marine mammals whose bodies contain traces of plastic"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"they\" in paragraph 6 refers to _______.", options: ["conventional plastic materials that do not break down under any natural conditions", "the proponents who claim that biodegradable materials offer significant environmental benefits", "industrial composting facilities that process organic and biodegradable waste", "biodegradable plastics that require industrial composting to decompose effectively"], answer: 3 }, { q: "What is the author's overall stance on solving the plastic pollution problem?", options: ["Cautious — the author recommends waiting for new materials technology before committing to major policy changes", "Idealistic — the author believes a single comprehensive global ban on plastic would resolve the crisis", "Pessimistic — the author implies that the problem has grown too large to be meaningfully reduced", "Pragmatic — the author argues that multiple coordinated strategies pursued at scale offer the best prospect for meaningful progress"], answer: 3 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added to paragraph 4? 'Marine biodiversity losses in heavily polluted areas further reduce the productive capacity of ocean ecosystems that fishing industries depend upon.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 1 }] },
    { title: "Water Pollution and the Global Environmental Crisis", paragraphs: ["Of all the environmental challenges confronting humanity in the twenty-first century, few carry consequences as immediate, as widespread, or as inequitably distributed as water pollution. [1] Unlike climate change — whose most catastrophic effects are projected to unfold over decades — contaminated water kills and disables people today, in every region of the world, with particular severity in communities that lack the infrastructure and institutional capacity to treat, monitor, and regulate their water supplies. [2] The World Health Organization estimates that unsafe water, sanitation, and hygiene collectively account for approximately 1.4 million deaths annually, the majority of them children under the age of five in low-income countries. [3] Yet water pollution is consistently underrepresented in global environmental governance relative to its human toll, arguably because its worst effects are concentrated among populations with limited political voice at the international level. [4]", "Water pollution arises from a wide and often interconnected range of sources. Agricultural runoff — carrying fertilisers, pesticides, and animal waste from farms into rivers, lakes, and groundwater — is the single largest contributor to water quality degradation globally. The nitrogen and phosphorus compounds in synthetic fertilisers, when they enter aquatic systems in excess, stimulate the explosive growth of algae and cyanobacteria in a process known as eutrophication. As these organisms die and decompose, bacteria consume the oxygen dissolved in the water, creating hypoxic or anoxic zones — areas of critically low or absent oxygen — in which most aquatic life cannot survive. Hundreds of such dead zones have been documented worldwide, including a large and persistent one at the mouth of the Mississippi River in the Gulf of Mexico, where agricultural drainage from America's vast Corn Belt creates conditions hostile to fish, shrimp, and the fishing industries that depend on them.", "Industrial discharges represent a second major category of water pollution, with consequences that are frequently more acutely toxic than those of agricultural contamination. Heavy metals — including mercury, lead, cadmium, and arsenic — enter water systems through mining operations, metal smelting, and the improper disposal of electronic waste. Unlike organic pollutants, which can sometimes be broken down by microbial activity, heavy metals persist indefinitely in aquatic sediments and accumulate in the tissues of organisms through a process called biomagnification, whereby concentrations increase at each successive step in the food chain. The top predators in contaminated aquatic ecosystems — including large fish, dolphins, and humans who consume them — may carry heavy metal concentrations in their tissues that are millions of times higher than the levels present in the surrounding water.", "Pharmaceutical contamination represents an emerging and poorly understood dimension of the water pollution problem. Residues of antibiotics, hormones, antidepressants, and other pharmaceutical compounds enter waterways through human and animal excretion, as well as through the improper disposal of unused medications. Conventional wastewater treatment plants were not designed to remove these compounds effectively, and studies in both high- and low-income countries have detected a wide range of pharmaceutical residues in rivers, lakes, and even drinking water supplies. The ecological implications are considerable: antibiotic residues in aquatic environments contribute to the development and spread of antibiotic-resistant bacteria, a phenomenon whose consequences for human medicine could prove as significant as the original infections that antibiotics were developed to treat. Endocrine-disrupting compounds — chemicals that interfere with hormonal signalling in animals — have been linked to reproductive abnormalities, population declines, and behavioural changes across a range of fish, amphibian, and reptile species.", "The governance of water pollution reflects the broader fragmentation of global environmental policy. Regulatory standards for water quality vary enormously between jurisdictions, and in many parts of the world the capacity to monitor and enforce those standards is severely constrained by limited institutional resources, corruption, and political pressure from the industries responsible for the most significant pollution. International agreements on transboundary water pollution — affecting rivers, lakes, and aquifers that cross national borders — are often weakly enforced and poorly coordinated. The Mekong River, shared by six countries in Southeast Asia, illustrates the governance challenge: upstream industrial and agricultural development has progressively altered the river's hydrology, sediment flow, and water quality in ways that affect the livelihoods of millions of people downstream, yet the institutional mechanisms for managing these shared impacts remain inadequate to the scale of the problem.", "Technological solutions to water pollution exist and, in many cases, are well understood. Advanced wastewater treatment technologies are capable of removing pharmaceutical residues, heavy metals, and a wide range of other contaminants from water supplies at municipal scale. Constructed wetlands and bioremediation techniques — which use plants, microorganisms, and natural ecological processes to filter and neutralise pollutants — offer lower-cost alternatives that are particularly well-suited to rural and low-income contexts. The constraint is not primarily technical but economic and political: deploying these technologies at the scale required to address the problem demands sustained investment, effective regulation, and the political will to prioritise water quality as a public good rather than treating it as a residual concern subordinated to agricultural and industrial productivity.", "Addressing water pollution equitably requires confronting the power asymmetries that have allowed it to persist. The communities most severely affected — subsistence farmers, artisanal fisherfolk, Indigenous peoples whose cultures and food systems depend on clean water, and urban poor in cities without functioning wastewater treatment — typically wield the least influence over the policies and corporate practices that determine the quality of their water. Meaningful progress will require not only technical investment and regulatory reform but also a fundamental reorientation of whose interests count in the governance of this most essential resource. The science of water pollution is, in most respects, well advanced; what has lagged is the political commitment to act on what that science so consistently and clearly demonstrates."], questions: [{ q: "Which of the following best captures the central argument of the passage?", options: ["The governance of water pollution has improved significantly in recent decades due to stronger international agreements", "Water pollution is primarily a technical problem that can be solved through investment in advanced treatment technology", "Agricultural runoff is more dangerous than industrial discharge and should be the exclusive focus of global water policy", "Water pollution is a severe and inequitably distributed global crisis whose persistence reflects political failures as much as technical ones"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"they\" in paragraph 2 refers to _______.", options: ["bacteria that consume oxygen as organic matter decomposes in water", "farms that release waste into rivers, lakes, and groundwater systems", "the nitrogen and phosphorus compounds found in synthetic fertilisers", "algae and cyanobacteria that grow rapidly in nutrient-rich water"], answer: 2 }, { q: "What can be inferred from paragraphs 2 and 3 together about the relative dangers of agricultural and industrial water pollution?", options: ["While agricultural runoff affects larger areas, industrial heavy metal pollution may be more acutely toxic and permanent due to biomagnification", "Industrial pollution is easier to regulate than agricultural runoff because it originates from identifiable fixed sources", "Both types of pollution are largely confined to rural areas and have little effect on urban water supplies", "Agricultural pollution is more dangerous than industrial pollution in every respect and in all contexts"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, why is antibiotic contamination of waterways considered particularly alarming?", options: ["Because antibiotic contamination has already caused the extinction of several freshwater species in regions with high pharmaceutical production", "Because antibiotics in water supplies directly poison fish and other aquatic organisms at concentrations too low for current monitoring to detect", "Because antibiotic residues in water promote the development of resistant bacteria, potentially undermining the effectiveness of medical treatments", "Because conventional wastewater treatment plants cannot remove antibiotics and governments have refused to invest in upgraded facilities"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what does the example of the Mekong River illustrate?", options: ["That the institutional mechanisms for managing shared international water resources are often inadequate to the scale of the problem", "That large international rivers are effectively self-cleaning and recover quickly from upstream pollution", "That Southeast Asian governments have been leaders in developing cooperative frameworks for transboundary water management", "That upstream countries bear sole legal responsibility for any water quality changes experienced by downstream nations"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, why are constructed wetlands and bioremediation techniques considered particularly suitable for certain contexts?", options: ["Because they require no ongoing maintenance once established, making them ideal for areas with limited institutional capacity", "Because they are lower in cost and therefore better suited to rural and low-income settings than advanced treatment plants", "Because they have been proven to outperform advanced wastewater treatment technologies across all measures of effectiveness", "Because they are the only methods capable of removing pharmaceutical residues and heavy metals from water supplies"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a group severely affected by water pollution?", options: ["Indigenous peoples whose food systems and cultural practices depend on clean water", "Industrial workers employed in factories located near contaminated rivers and lakes", "Subsistence farmers who rely on clean water sources for their crops and livestock", "Urban poor in cities that lack functioning wastewater treatment infrastructure"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"persist\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["increase rapidly in concentration as they travel downstream through river systems", "remain present without breaking down over an extended period of time", "become less harmful as they are absorbed into the tissues of aquatic organisms", "dissolve gradually into surrounding water through natural chemical reactions"], answer: 1 }, { q: "Which of the following best characterises the author's overall attitude toward the global response to water pollution?", options: ["Technically focused — the author argues that developing better treatment technologies is the only meaningful path forward", "Historically oriented — the author traces the origins of water governance failures to specific decisions made in the twentieth century", "Critically engaged — the author acknowledges the availability of solutions but argues that political will and equity have consistently failed to match the scientific evidence", "Broadly satisfied — the author believes current international frameworks are adequate but need minor improvements"], answer: 2 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added to paragraph 1? 'This disparity in impact is not accidental; it reflects deep structural inequalities in which communities bear the cost of pollution generated primarily for the economic benefit of others.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 3 }] },
  ] },
  { id: "rd-128", num: 128, title: "Đề 128", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "Why Opposites May Attract: DNA and Human Attraction", paragraphs: ["For many years, scientists have wondered why some couples seem to be drawn to each other almost instantly, while others take much longer to feel a connection. One interesting theory suggests that our genes, the tiny units of DNA that carry information about who we are, might play a hidden role in choosing a partner. According to this idea, people may be naturally attracted to partners whose immune-system genes are quite different from their own, rather than similar.", "The genes at the center of this theory are part of a group called the Major Histocompatibility Complex, often shortened to MHC. These genes help the body's immune system recognize harmful invaders such as bacteria and viruses. Because MHC genes vary so much from person to person, researchers became curious about whether people could somehow sense these differences in a potential partner without even realizing it.", "In a well-known experiment, women were asked to smell T-shirts that had been worn by different men for two nights in a row, without knowing which shirt belonged to which man. Time after time, most women reported that the shirts worn by men with different MHC genes smelled more pleasant than the shirts worn by men whose MHC genes were similar to their own. Researchers concluded that people might unconsciously use scent to judge genetic compatibility , even though almost none of the participants could explain exactly why one shirt smelled better than another.", "From a biological point of view, this pattern makes a certain kind of sense. When two partners have different MHC genes, their children may inherit a wider range of immune defenses, which could make those children better able to fight off diseases. In other words, this kind of genetic diversity could offer a survival advantage that has been shaped by evolution over an extremely long period of time. Some scientists believe that our ancestors developed a sensitivity to these genetic differences long before anyone understood what DNA actually was.", "Despite this evidence, not everyone finds the theory fully convincing . Critics point out that human attraction is influenced by many other factors as well, including shared values, similar interests, upbringing, and simple daily habits, and that no single explanation can account for something as complicated as love. Some studies conducted in different countries have also produced mixed or unclear results, with a few failing to find the same pattern that was seen in the original T-shirt experiment. Cultural background and personal experience, researchers note, may sometimes shift these preferences in ways that are hard to measure precisely.", "Even so, most researchers agree the theory is worth taking seriously, since it connects two areas that once seemed completely separate: the science of the immune system and the everyday mystery of falling in love. Continued research into this area may eventually reveal even more surprising links between our biology and the choices we make in relationships."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["A theory suggests that people may be unconsciously attracted to partners with different immune-system genes, though other factors also shape attraction.", "MHC genes are the newest medical treatment for immune system diseases.", "The T-shirt experiment showed that all women prefer the same type of scent regardless of genetics.", "Scientists have proven that scent is the only factor that determines human attraction."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what does the abbreviation MHC stand for?", options: ["Mutual Health Compatibility", "Major Histocompatibility Complex", "Molecular Heredity Center", "Modern Human Chromosome"], answer: 1 }, { q: "In the T-shirt experiment described in the passage, what were women asked to do?", options: ["Smell T-shirts worn by different men for two nights in a row", "Answer a written survey about their ideal partner", "Meet several men in person and choose a favorite", "Rate photographs of different men based on attractiveness"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what possible advantage might children gain when their parents have different MHC genes?", options: ["A wider range of immune defenses against diseases", "Faster physical growth during childhood", "A higher chance of developing artistic talent", "A stronger sense of smell than other children"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a factor influencing human attraction?", options: ["Shared values", "Body height", "Similar interests", "Upbringing"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"those\" in paragraph 4 refers to _______.", options: ["genes", "children", "scientists", "partners"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"compatibility\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["suitability", "curiosity", "popularity", "sensitivity"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"convincing\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["complicated", "accurate", "persuasive", "popular"], answer: 2 }, { q: "Based on the passage, what can be inferred about why results on this theory differ across countries?", options: ["Cultural background and personal experience may influence how strongly these genetic preferences appear", "Scientists in different countries used completely different types of T-shirts", "MHC genes do not exist in people outside of the original study's country", "Women in other countries refused to take part in similar experiments"], answer: 0 }, { q: "What is the author's attitude toward the MHC attraction theory, as expressed in the passage?", options: ["Completely dismissive, treating the theory as scientifically baseless", "Open-minded but balanced, presenting the theory as worth taking seriously while acknowledging valid criticism", "Indifferent, showing no real interest in whether the theory is true or not", "Fully convinced, presenting the theory as an established scientific fact with no doubts"], answer: 1 }] },
    { title: "Rethinking Mental Health in the Modern Workplace", paragraphs: ["Mental health has moved from being a largely private concern to a subject openly discussed in workplaces, schools, and public policy debates around the world. This shift reflects a broader change in how societies understand psychological well-being: rather than treating mental health as simply the absence of diagnosed illness, many experts now describe it as an ongoing state that everyone actively manages, much like physical fitness. Surveys conducted across several countries suggest that a growing share of employees consider workplace mental health support to be as important as salary when choosing where to work.", "Employers have responded to this shift in varied ways. Some companies have introduced formal wellness programs, offering employees access to counseling services, mental health days, and training sessions designed to help managers recognize early signs of burnout among their staff. Other organizations have taken a less structured approach, focusing instead on cultivating an open culture where employees feel comfortable discussing stress without fear of professional consequences. Advocates of these programs argue that supporting mental health is not merely compassionate but also economically sensible, since untreated stress and burnout are strongly associated with reduced productivity and higher staff turnover.", "Nevertheless, measuring whether these workplace initiatives actually improve mental health outcomes has proven surprisingly difficult. Several large-scale reviews have found that many popular interventions, such as brief mindfulness workshops or one-off stress-management seminars, produce only modest and often short-lived improvements, particularly when the underlying causes of workplace stress, such as excessive workloads or poor management practices, remain unaddressed. Some researchers argue that placing the primary responsibility on individual employees to manage their own stress may quietly shift attention away from structural problems that organizations themselves should be expected to fix.", "Cultural attitudes toward mental health also continue to vary considerably between and even within countries, which complicates any single, universal approach to workplace support. In some professional environments, openly discussing psychological struggles remains associated with weakness or unreliability, discouraging employees from seeking help even when formal support programs are technically available to them. In other settings, greater openness has taken hold more quickly, particularly among younger employees, who tend to view conversations about mental health as a normal and even expected part of professional life rather than something to be hidden. This generational divide sometimes creates friction between employees and more traditionally minded managers, who may misinterpret openness about mental health as unprofessional.", "Beyond formal programs, informal workplace relationships appear to play a significant role in shaping employees' psychological well-being. Employees who report feeling genuinely supported by close colleagues tend to show greater resilience when facing difficult periods at work, according to several long-term workplace studies, even in organizations that lack extensive formal mental health resources. This finding has led some researchers to argue that fostering strong interpersonal trust among coworkers may be at least as valuable as introducing new official policies, since such relationships often continue to function even when management support proves inconsistent or unreliable.", "Critics of the current corporate enthusiasm for mental health initiatives caution that some programs function mainly as public relations exercises rather than as genuine efforts to address underlying problems. They point to cases where companies promote mental health awareness campaigns while simultaneously maintaining demanding workloads or precarious employment conditions that arguably undermine the very well-being such campaigns claim to support. This apparent contradiction, critics argue, may not necessarily reflect deliberate hypocrisy on the part of employers, but rather a persistent gap between recognizing a problem and having the organizational will, or resources, to address its root causes.", "Looking forward, many workplace psychologists argue that meaningful progress will require organizations to move beyond isolated wellness initiatives and instead reconsider more fundamental aspects of how work itself is structured, including staffing levels, management training, and realistic expectations around workload. Whether this deeper structural shift will occur, or whether mental health support will remain largely confined to supplementary programs layered on top of unchanged working conditions, remains an open question that will likely shape workplace culture for years to come."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Companies that avoid discussing mental health always have higher employee turnover than those that do.", "Younger employees are less capable of handling workplace stress than older employees.", "Mindfulness workshops have been scientifically proven to eliminate workplace stress entirely.", "Workplace mental health support has become more common, but its actual effectiveness remains uncertain and dependent on addressing deeper structural issues."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what do formal workplace wellness programs sometimes offer employees?", options: ["Free housing near the workplace", "Counseling services, mental health days, and manager training sessions", "Guaranteed promotions for employees who report stress", "Mandatory unpaid leave for all staff members"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what have several large-scale reviews found about interventions such as mindfulness workshops?", options: ["They were rejected by most employees as unhelpful from the very beginning", "They completely eliminate the need for any other form of mental health support", "They produce only modest and often short-lived improvements, especially when underlying causes of stress remain unaddressed", "They are more effective than long-term structural changes to workload"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what tends to happen for employees who feel genuinely supported by close colleagues?", options: ["They tend to leave their jobs more frequently than other employees", "They are less likely to be promoted compared with employees who rely on formal programs", "They show greater resilience during difficult periods, even in organizations lacking extensive formal mental health resources", "They no longer require any workplace wellness programs at all"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as an approach employers have taken toward workplace mental health?", options: ["Cultivating an open culture around discussing stress", "Requiring unpaid leave for all staff", "Offering counseling services", "Training managers to recognize signs of burnout"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"undermine\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["measure", "expose", "weaken", "promote"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"precarious\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["unstable", "temporary", "generous", "formal"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The phrase \"such relationships\" in paragraph 5 refers to _______.", options: ["generational differences", "management training programs", "official mental health policies", "supportive relationships between coworkers"], answer: 3 }, { q: "Based on the passage, what can be inferred about the future effectiveness of workplace mental health initiatives?", options: ["Meaningful progress may depend less on adding more standalone wellness programs and more on restructuring workload, staffing, and management practices", "All companies will eventually abandon wellness programs because they have been proven completely ineffective", "Younger employees will refuse to work for companies that offer any wellness programs", "Cultural attitudes toward mental health are expected to become identical across all countries within a few years"], answer: 0 }, { q: "What is the author's overall attitude toward corporate mental health initiatives, as expressed in the passage?", options: ["Indifferent, expressing no clear position on whether these programs matter", "Entirely dismissive, treating all workplace mental health programs as public relations exercises", "Uncritically enthusiastic, presenting all wellness programs as fully effective", "Cautiously supportive, recognizing genuine progress while questioning whether current efforts address root causes"], answer: 3 }] },
    { title: "Disneyland and the Architecture of Manufactured Nostalgia", paragraphs: ["When Disneyland opened in Anaheim, California, in 1955, it represented something genuinely unprecedented in the history of leisure: an entire commercial environment engineered from the ground up to produce a specific emotional experience in visitors, rather than simply providing rides or attractions in the manner of a traditional amusement park. Walt Disney reportedly conceived the idea after visiting conventional amusement parks with his daughters and finding them dirty, poorly maintained, and lacking any coherent sense of atmosphere. His solution was to design a park in which every visible surface, from the pavement texture to the uniforms worn by staff, was deliberately controlled to reinforce a unified theme, a level of environmental control that had no real precedent in commercial entertainment at the time.", "Central to this design philosophy was what Disney's team called \"weenies,\" internal industry slang for large visual landmarks, such as Sleeping Beauty Castle, positioned to draw visitors intuitively through the park without the need for explicit signage. This technique, borrowed loosely from theatrical set design, reflected a broader principle: visitors should feel as though they are moving through a continuous narrative rather than a disconnected collection of attractions. Land areas such as Frontierland, Adventureland, and Tomorrowland were each assigned a distinct architectural vocabulary, sound design, and even scent profile, an approach that later came to be studied by academics as an early and unusually sophisticated example of what is now called experiential branding.", "Despite its enormous commercial success, Disneyland has long attracted scholarly criticism, particularly from cultural theorists who argue that the park exemplifies a broader phenomenon of manufactured, sanitized nostalgia. Main Street USA, the park's entrance area, was modeled loosely on small-town America of the early twentieth century, yet critics point out that this version of the past conspicuously omits the poverty, racial segregation, and social conflict that also characterized that period. Such critics contend that the emotional comfort many visitors report feeling on Main Street is not an accident but rather the product of a carefully curated historical fiction, one designed to feel authentic precisely because its inconvenient details have been quietly removed.", "Defenders of the park counter that this criticism, while not without some merit, arguably misunderstands the fundamental purpose of the space. Disneyland was never intended, they argue, to function as a museum offering rigorous historical accuracy; rather, it was conceived explicitly as an idealized emotional environment, and judging it by the standards of historical documentation may therefore be applying an inappropriate framework to what is, at its core, a theatrical experience. This defense does not necessarily resolve the underlying tension, however, since the park's own marketing has at times blurred this distinction, occasionally describing its recreated environments in language that implies a kind of historical authenticity the spaces do not actually possess.", "The park's operational systems have proven nearly as influential as its aesthetic design. Disneyland pioneered numerous crowd-management techniques, including switchback queue lines designed to make waiting feel shorter than it actually is, and elaborate backstage tunnel systems allowing costumed staff to move between different themed lands without disrupting visitors' sense of immersion by appearing out of context. These operational innovations were subsequently studied and adopted across the broader hospitality and retail industries, extending Disneyland's influence considerably beyond the specific context of theme parks. It would be a considerable understatement to describe the tunnel system merely as a maintenance convenience, since it also functions as a deliberate mechanism for preserving the emotional coherence the park's designers worked so carefully to construct.", "Economically, Disneyland also helped establish a business model, sometimes described as \"synergy,\" in which a single fictional universe could be monetized simultaneously across films, merchandise, television, and physical parks, each component reinforcing consumer engagement with the others. This integrated approach, radical when first attempted in the 1950s, has since become standard practice throughout the global entertainment industry, adopted by numerous competitors and imitators. Some media economists argue that Disneyland should therefore be understood not merely as a park but as the founding prototype of an entire commercial strategy that now extends across contemporary streaming platforms, sports franchises, and branded consumer products worldwide.", "Taken together, these design, operational, and economic innovations help explain why Disneyland continues to attract sustained academic attention more than seven decades after its opening. Whether viewed primarily as an ingenious feat of environmental design or as a troubling exercise in commercialized nostalgia, the park remains a rare case in which a single physical space fundamentally reshaped both the entertainment industry's business practices and its underlying attitude toward the immersive presentation of history and place."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["The tunnel system beneath Disneyland was the single most important factor in the park's commercial success.", "Disneyland's business model failed to influence any other companies in the entertainment industry.", "Disneyland's design, operational, and economic innovations reshaped the entertainment industry, even as scholars continue to debate its portrayal of nostalgia and history.", "Disneyland was primarily created to serve as an accurate historical museum of early twentieth-century America."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what was the purpose of the landmarks Disney's team called \"weenies\"?", options: ["To serve primarily as photo locations for visiting families", "To draw visitors intuitively through the park without the need for explicit signage, reflecting a broader narrative-driven design philosophy", "To reduce the total cost of constructing the park", "To mark the boundaries between different themed lands using explicit signage"], answer: 1 }, { q: "Based on the passage, what do critics argue about Main Street USA's portrayal of early twentieth-century America?", options: ["It was modeled on a specific real town that no longer exists today", "It was redesigned in response to direct criticism from historians shortly after the park opened", "It presents a curated version of the past that omits difficult social realities, producing a comforting but historically incomplete fiction", "It fully documents the poverty and social conflict of the era for educational purposes"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, how does the \"synergy\" business model function?", options: ["Each Disney product line operates independently with no connection to any other division", "Competing theme parks are prevented from using similar business strategies", "Backstage tunnels allow costumed staff to move between themed lands without disrupting visitor immersion", "A single fictional universe is monetized simultaneously across films, merchandise, television, and physical parks, with each component reinforcing the others"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as an operational or design innovation introduced by Disneyland?", options: ["Distinct architectural vocabularies for different themed lands", "An electronic ticketing system that eliminated the need for any physical queue lines", "Backstage tunnel systems", "Switchback queue lines"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"curated\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["carefully selected", "financially supported", "completely invented", "publicly criticized"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"immersion\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["deep engagement", "financial investment", "historical accuracy", "physical construction"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The phrase \"this defense\" in paragraph 4 refers to _______.", options: ["the park's marketing language", "the claim that Main Street USA omits poverty and segregation", "the argument that Disneyland was never meant to function as a historically accurate museum", "the criticism made by cultural theorists"], answer: 2 }, { q: "What can be inferred about the tension between Disneyland's design purpose and its public perception?", options: ["The park's designers intended Main Street USA to be a precise historical replica from the very beginning", "Even if Disneyland was conceived purely as a theatrical, idealized space rather than a historical record, its own marketing has sometimes blurred this distinction, which keeps the criticism from being fully dismissed by the theatrical-space defense", "The tension is fully resolved because Disneyland's marketing never suggests any degree of historical authenticity", "Cultural theorists agree entirely with Disneyland's defenders and no longer criticize the park's portrayal of history"], answer: 1 }, { q: "What is the author's overall purpose in presenting both the design achievements and the scholarly criticism of Disneyland?", options: ["To argue that Disneyland's critics have no valid points worth considering", "To promote Disneyland as a flawless historical museum", "To provide a balanced academic account that recognizes Disneyland's genuine innovations while taking seriously the legitimate critiques of its portrayal of history", "To focus exclusively on the park's financial performance while ignoring design and cultural questions"], answer: 2 }] },
    { title: "The Leopard's Quiet Success: Ecological Flexibility Under Pressure", paragraphs: ["Among the world's large cats, the leopard occupies a curious and somewhat paradoxical position: it is simultaneously one of the most widely distributed of the big cats, ranging historically across vast portions of Africa and Asia, and yet one of the least studied relative to its more charismatic relatives, the lion and the tiger. This relative scientific neglect arguably stems not from any lack of ecological significance but rather from the leopard's famously elusive , largely solitary, and predominantly nocturnal behavior, which makes sustained field observation considerably more difficult than studying species that live and hunt in more visible social groups. Not only does this secrecy complicate research, but it may also partly explain why leopard population trends have historically received less public attention than the more dramatic declines documented among lions.", "What arguably distinguishes the leopard most sharply from other big cats is not raw physical power, since it is generally smaller and less powerful than either lions or tigers, but rather an unusual degree of ecological and behavioral flexibility. Leopards inhabit environments ranging from dense rainforest to arid semi-desert, from high-altitude mountain terrain to the immediate outskirts of major cities, a range of habitat tolerance essentially unmatched among the world's large carnivores. This adaptability extends to diet as well; while lions and tigers tend toward comparatively specialized prey preferences, leopards have been documented consuming prey ranging from small insects and rodents to antelope several times their own body weight, a dietary breadth that likely underlies much of their persistence in landscapes where other large predators have already disappeared.", "This flexibility does not, however, imply that leopard populations face no serious threats, nor should it be taken to suggest that the species is thriving uniformly across its range. Genetic and field studies indicate that leopard populations have contracted significantly across large portions of their historical range, particularly in North Africa and parts of Asia, even as certain other populations, especially in fragmented but well-protected areas of southern Africa, have remained comparatively stable. This uneven pattern complicates any simple narrative about the species' overall conservation status; a leopard population's resilience in one particular region does not necessarily indicate that the species as a whole is secure, and conservationists caution against extrapolating from isolated success stories to broader continental trends.", "One especially striking illustration of leopard adaptability involves the species' documented capacity to persist in landscapes heavily modified by human activity, including agricultural areas and, in certain well-documented cases, the immediate periphery of large cities. Research conducted near Mumbai, India, has documented a resident leopard population living within a protected forest patch surrounded almost entirely by dense urban development, a scenario that would be essentially unthinkable for most other large carnivores. This proximity to human settlement, however, arguably creates as many problems as it resolves; while it demonstrates the leopard's remarkable behavioral plasticity , it also generates recurring human-wildlife conflict, including occasional livestock predation and, less frequently, direct encounters with people, outcomes that complicate local communities' willingness to tolerate the species' continued presence nearby.", "The scientific literature on human-leopard conflict resists any simple, uniform conclusion. Some researchers argue that conflict incidents are driven primarily by the depletion of natural prey populations, which forces leopards toward livestock and, occasionally, human settlements out of ecological necessity rather than genuine behavioral preference. Other researchers contend that conflict may not necessarily imply prey scarcity at all, since leopards in certain well-studied regions with abundant wild prey nonetheless continue to take livestock opportunistically, suggesting the behavior may sometimes reflect efficient foraging strategy rather than desperation. This does not mean prey depletion is irrelevant, but rather that no single explanation adequately accounts for the full range of documented conflict patterns across the leopard's extraordinarily varied geographic range.", "Conservation strategies have accordingly shifted away from approaches designed for more socially visible, wide-ranging species such as lions, toward approaches better suited to a solitary, adaptable predator capable of persisting in fragmented and human-dominated landscapes. Rather than focusing exclusively on large, contiguous protected areas, some conservationists now argue for strategies emphasizing corridor connectivity between fragmented habitat patches, community-based livestock protection measures, and localized conflict-mitigation programs tailored to specific regional circumstances. Critics of this shift , however, caution that emphasizing the leopard's adaptability could inadvertently encourage complacency among policymakers, who might reasonably but mistakenly assume, given the species' documented resilience elsewhere, that leopard populations require comparatively little dedicated conservation investment.", "Ultimately, the leopard's ecological story resists easy categorization as either a straightforward conservation success or an unambiguous crisis. Its documented adaptability does not guarantee long-term persistence, particularly as habitat fragmentation and human population growth continue to intensify across much of its range, nor does regional population decline necessarily indicate that the species as a whole faces imminent extinction. This very ambiguity, rather than undermining the case for continued research, arguably strengthens it: understanding precisely which forms of adaptability allow leopards to persist, and under what specific ecological and social conditions that adaptability eventually fails, may prove essential to conserving not only leopards themselves but other adaptable species facing comparable pressures worldwide."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Leopard populations are uniformly stable and require no further conservation attention anywhere in their range.", "Human-leopard conflict is caused exclusively by the depletion of natural prey populations.", "The leopard's unusual ecological and behavioral flexibility allows it to persist across highly varied and often human-dominated landscapes, but this adaptability does not guarantee the species' long-term security everywhere.", "Leopards are less powerful than lions and tigers and are therefore of little ecological interest to researchers."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what distinguishes leopards most sharply from lions and tigers?", options: ["Their greater physical size and raw power", "Their exclusively nocturnal hunting behavior, which lions and tigers do not share", "Their tendency to live and hunt in large, visible social groups", "An unusual degree of ecological and behavioral flexibility, including habitat tolerance and dietary breadth"], answer: 3 }, { q: "Based on the passage, what does the research conducted near Mumbai illustrate?", options: ["That leopards can persist in a protected patch surrounded by dense urban development, though this proximity also generates recurring conflict with nearby communities", "That urban leopard populations face no conflict with local communities whatsoever", "That the Mumbai leopard population is the largest in the world", "That leopards near Mumbai have completely abandoned hunting wild prey in favor of livestock"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, how have conservation strategies for leopards shifted?", options: ["Toward focusing solely on captive breeding programs", "Toward eliminating all human settlement near leopard habitats", "Toward relying exclusively on large, contiguous protected areas modeled on lion conservation", "Away from approaches designed for wide-ranging, socially visible species, toward corridor connectivity, community-based livestock protection, and localized conflict mitigation"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a consequence or aspect of leopards living near human settlements?", options: ["Complete elimination of all wild prey species in the surrounding region", "Reduced local tolerance for the species' presence", "Direct encounters with people", "Occasional livestock predation"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"elusive\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["hard to observe", "extremely aggressive", "poorly nourished", "widely distributed"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"plasticity\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["adaptability", "aggression", "geographic isolation", "population size"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The phrase \"this shift\" in paragraph 6 refers to _______.", options: ["the move away from lion-style conservation approaches toward strategies suited to a solitary, adaptable predator", "the disagreement among researchers about the causes of human-leopard conflict", "the decline of leopard populations in North Africa", "the growth of urban development near Mumbai"], answer: 0 }, { q: "What can be inferred about the relationship between prey scarcity and human-leopard conflict?", options: ["Prey depletion can contribute to conflict, but since leopards in prey-rich regions also take livestock opportunistically, scarcity alone does not necessarily explain every documented conflict pattern", "Researchers have reached full agreement that conflict is caused exclusively by efficient foraging strategy rather than necessity", "Leopards in regions with abundant wild prey never interact with livestock under any circumstances", "Prey scarcity is the only documented cause of livestock predation by leopards"], answer: 0 }, { q: "How does the author primarily structure the argument across the passage regarding leopard adaptability and conservation status?", options: ["By presenting only the views of critics who oppose current conservation strategies, without offering counterarguments", "By arguing in a straightforward, linear fashion that leopards face no meaningful conservation threats anywhere", "By focusing the entire argument on comparing leopard physical strength to that of lions and tigers", "By repeatedly presenting evidence of the leopard's flexibility alongside explicit caution that this flexibility does not equate to guaranteed long-term security, building toward a conclusion that resists simple categorization"], answer: 3 }] },
  ] },
];
